#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551469} :activity/id #uuid "22ea1569-895b-50ca-99d6-870b4789bc76" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049605 :topic/id #uuid "03eacd71-e195-528e-bb29-8b23c4e95ba7" :topic/math-academy-id 971} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63016c40-26b8-54f1-af02-fe4df536e028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.044" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436173} {:answer/id #uuid "9e6154ce-f843-5fdc-a8b0-5dfb33599b4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.058" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436185} {:answer/id #uuid "cc67574e-605f-521d-93f0-a43b7eb005d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.541" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436195} {:answer/id #uuid "e6bab3ee-bcc0-51a4-967f-ae1ffb30e505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.056" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436204} {:answer/id #uuid "efcedd14-6e3b-5303-8273-c5f15c5397fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.054" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436206}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436173} :answer-field/id #uuid "efcaebab-808b-586a-b686-8d0602967092" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "296a9d4d-087e-5552-b718-aa436d5fbac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143748" :question/problem "A company produces replacement batteries for a particular cell phone model at three locations. Location $A$ produces $30%$ of the batteries, of which $3%$ are defective. Location $B$ produces $38%$ of the batteries, of which $5%$ are defective. And location $C$  produces $32%$ of the batteries, of which also $5%$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that the battery is defective, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the battery was produced at location $A,$\n\n- let $B$  be the event that the battery was produced at location $B,$\n\n- let $C$  be the event that the battery was produced at location $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since  location $A$ produces $30%$ of the batteries,  location $B$ produces $38%$ of the batteries, and location $C$ produces $32%$ of the batteries, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.3,\\,P(B)=0.38,\\,P(C)=0.32.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of the batteries produced at location $A$, $5%$ of the batteries produced at location $B$, and $5%$ of the batteries produced at  location $C,$ are defective, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.03,\\,P(D|B)=0.05,\\,P(D|C)=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.03)(0.3)+(0.05)(0.38)+(0.05)(0.32) \\\\  & =0.009+0.019+0.016 \\\\  & =0.044.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "015fd7cc-296c-527b-8d93-783a3144a040" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551390}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551383 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551304 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18462a6e-e5aa-5da9-9169-1897af9ddfc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "74%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550421} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd5ac84-c3f0-5b1e-9dc6-1b0238589e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "71%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550549} {:answer/id #uuid "88aaed32-cbc4-5fb1-b688-7cc95b6f62b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "59%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550715} {:answer/id #uuid "956129cb-062c-52cb-8b95-1feac6e6425c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "79%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550743} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5f37d0-df7f-5e60-8207-efacf2cdc514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550924}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550549} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2414de2-0b71-5f21-ba04-5ccbaa91ec84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "87509e91-72b0-5f1f-83b2-ad41631e8459" :question/math-academy-id "q-144037" :question/problem "A cattle rancher has three ranches, where $34%$ of the rancher's cows are on ranch $A$, $36%$ are on ranch $B,$ and $30%$ on ranch $C.$ If $25%$ of all cows on ranch $A$ and $20%$ of all cows on ranch $B$ are dairy cows, and the probability that a randomly selected cow from any of the three ranches is a dairy cow is $37%,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected cow from ranch $C$ is a dairy cow?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a selected cow is a dairy cow, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the cow is from ranch $A$,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the cow is from ranch $B$,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the cow is from ranch $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that the randomly selected cow is a dairy cow, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since $34%$ of all the rancher's cows are on ranch $A$, $36%$ are on ranch $B,$ and $30%$ are on ranch $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.34,\\,P(B)=0.36,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of all cows on ranch $A$ and $20%$ of all cows on ranch $B$ are dairy cows, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.25,\\,P(D|B)=0.2.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected cow from any of the three ranches is  a dairy cow is $37%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.37.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.37 & =(0.25)(0.34)+(0.2)(0.36)+P(D|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.37 & =0.085+0.072+0.3P(D|C) \\\\ 0.3P(D|C) & =0.213 \\\\ P(D|C) & =0.71\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected cow from ranch $C$ is a dairy cow is $71%.$"} :step/id #uuid "330a5393-8793-5481-819f-7f472b79e0cb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a66e4d-d36b-5589-9e76-8d2fc1124283" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.525" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436146} {:answer/id #uuid "32133a22-82bc-5a7c-9eac-727b8484e3b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.515" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436154} {:answer/id #uuid "357f442f-a27d-5c2b-8c03-4173f9ed1135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.555" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436158} {:answer/id #uuid "35872c71-32ca-5629-8a73-9868fcb5d5a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.625" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436159} {:answer/id #uuid "82363ae0-ea2c-5ea2-bd36-f1a97cf797bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.575" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436178}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436178} :answer-field/id #uuid "e132532a-be97-5775-bb17-2406d8caeef2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7c588063-b6d3-5525-ae01-ed797a4d9cc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143753" :question/problem "In a gas station, $40%$ of the clients buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ buy Supreme gasoline. If $65%$ of the clients that purchase Regular gasoline paid with a credit card, $50%$ of the clients that buy Premium gasoline paid with a credit card, and $60%$ of the clients that buy Supreme gasoline paid with a credit card,  what is the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card?" :question/worked-solution "Let  $C$ be the event that the client paid with a credit card, and\n\n- let $R$ be the event that the client bought Regular gasoline,\n\n- let $Q$  be the event that the client bought Premium gasoline,\n\n- let $S$ be the event that the client bought Supreme gasoline.\n\nThen the disjoint union $R⊔Q⊔S$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap R)+P(C\\cap Q)+P(C\\cap S) \\\\  & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of the clients buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ of the clients buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ of the clients buy Supreme gasoline, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.4,\\,P(Q)=0.45,\\,P(S)=0.15.\n$$\n\n- Since $65%$ of the clients that buy Regular gasoline, $50%$ of the clients that buy Premium gasoline, and $60%$ of the clients that buy Supreme gasoline,  paid with a credit card, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.65,\\,P(C|Q)=0.5,\\,P(C|S)=0.6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S) \\\\  & =(0.65)(0.4)+(0.5)(0.45)+(0.6)(0.15) \\\\  & =0.26+0.225+0.009 \\\\  & =0.575.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3ab20fd2-6a65-50a2-902a-224e12d123dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551437}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551437 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49d67ba6-1b1f-58fa-a1c0-e61e944c3560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.044" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436163} {:answer/id #uuid "82276f72-0877-5798-8fe1-76ed94a97b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436177} {:answer/id #uuid "9482cc37-4f52-55f0-820b-76e7d8d26527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.038" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436182} {:answer/id #uuid "e4fcf1de-ebb1-592d-9f04-b9c050c3551b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.0052" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436203} {:answer/id #uuid "ea852479-a868-5154-ba52-8c97031f5080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436205}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436205} :answer-field/id #uuid "e674252e-02a4-53ae-adde-553bbd0c47c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "199cacb7-9e7a-5d07-af35-fc181af78213" :question/math-academy-id "q-143872" :question/problem "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the component population, $45%$ are produced by machine $A,$  $25%$ by machine $B,$ and $30%$ by machine $C.$ If $5%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ and $3%$ of those produced by machine $B$ are defective, and the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is $0.042,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $C$ is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected component is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $45%$ of the components, machine $B$ produces $25%$ of the components, and machine $C$ produces $30%$ of the components, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.45,\\,P(B)=0.25,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $5%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ and $3%$ of the components produced by machine $B$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.05,\\,P(D|B)=0.03\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is $0.042,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.042.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.042 & =(0.05)(0.45)+(0.03)(0.25)+P(D|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.042 & =0.0225+0.0075+0.3P(D|C) \\\\ 0.3P(D|C) & =0.012 \\\\ P(D|C) & =0.04\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $C$ is defective is $0.04.$"} :step/id #uuid "b031a733-e72b-5ee4-a3be-0e48bbdb6dfb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551383}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551469 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2091824b-6f43-593c-84fe-25de29e7e4e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{85}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550441} {:answer/id #uuid "4da788ef-fc41-5934-85c7-6c54e9d66986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{63}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550552} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9ac3d1-fd3a-5663-99f5-0b6e86c9d756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{90}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550690} {:answer/id #uuid "8a76f1ca-01f8-5917-8e75-64c4de462b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{38}{165}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550717} {:answer/id #uuid "8e788705-609a-5a7a-af02-0595198b67ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{113}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550731}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550690} :answer-field/id #uuid "be770eee-142d-5138-82b0-d774ea34700b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a3aefa5f-7730-50af-bdda-78228d7a1e41" :question/math-academy-id "q-143572" :question/problem "In a hardware store, there are three boxes containing light bulbs. Box $A$ contains $15$ bulbs, of which $2$ are defective. Box $B$ contains $10$ bulbs, of which $1$ is defective. And box $C$ contains $10$ light bulbs, of which $2$ are defective. A box is selected at random, and a bulb is randomly selected from the box. Find the probability that the bulb is defective." :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event of selecting a defective bulb, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the bulb is from box $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the bulb is from box $B$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the bulb is from box $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space. \n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a defective bulb at random from one of the three boxes, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a box is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $2$ defective bulbs out of $15$  in box $A,$ $1$ defective bulb out of $10$ in box $B,$ and $2$  defective bulbs out of $10$ bulbs in box $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=\\frac{2}{15},\\,P(D|B)=\\frac{1}{10},\\,P(D|C)=\\frac{2}{10}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability of selecting a defective bulb at random from one of the three boxes is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{15})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{10})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{2}{10})(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{45}+\\frac{1}{30}+\\frac{2}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{90}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f9f7759e-a162-5e5d-8af0-e5c76d19e07c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551358}}] :activity/title "Extending the Law of Total Probability" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550344}]}
